<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George II, Duke of Saxe-Meiningen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1826–1914</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Theatre Duke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George II, Duke of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, came to power in 1866 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> deposed his father for siding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Known as the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> duke', he directed his court company himself, and the Meiningen troupe's historically accurate staging and ensemble acting influenced theatre across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He also supported the court orchestra under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-von-bulow">Hans von Bülow</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-ii-duke-of-saxe-meiningen.jpg" alt="George II, Duke of Saxe-Meiningen" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Person · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herzog_Georg_II_von_Saxe_Meiningen_(10).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
